=begin
      author: Fizzleworth
    co-writer: Claude (Anthropic)
        name: undead_bounty
        game: gemstone
        tags: bounty, adventurer's guild, undead, voln
    requires: Lich > 5.4.0
     version: 1.5

  Want to keep earning favor with Voln while at the same time completing bounties?

  Then this is your script!

  Automates the "reroll until it's undead" bounty loop:
    1. Go to the Adventurer's Guild and ask the bounty master for a new
       kill/dangerous bounty (`ask taskmaster for bounty <type>`).
    2. Figure out which town the assignment sent you to (Bounty.current.town),
       find that town's guard room by map tag, and `ask <guard> for bounty`
       to learn which creature you've been assigned.
    3. Check whether that creature is undead (via the same gameobj-data
       type lookup ;ebestiary uses).
       - If it IS undead, stop so you can take over.
       - If it is NOT undead, go back to the guild, `ask taskmaster for
         removal` (twice, to confirm), and loop.

  This works across any town without per-town configuration: the bounty
  master is always named "Taskmaster" (except aboard Sailor's Grief, which
  isn't handled here), and the guard's room is found generically via the
  map database's 'advguard'/'advguard2' tags nearest the assigned town -
  the same technique ebounty.lic uses. The guard's title also varies by
  town (guard, sergeant, sentry, tavernkeeper, purser, etc.), so that's
  matched with a broad pattern instead of a fixed word.

  Also handles a few real-world hiccups along the way: it retries if the
  taskmaster is busy or says you already have a task assigned (requesting
  removal first), and it waits for a sleeping town guard to wake up instead
  of giving up.

  usage:
    ;undead_bounty help                  - show this help
    ;undead_bounty kill                  - loop requesting "kill" (culling) bounties,
                                            stop on any undead assignment
    ;undead_bounty dangerous             - same, but for "dangerous" bounties
    ;undead_bounty kill tomb wight       - stop only when the assigned creature is
                                            undead AND matches "tomb wight" (partial,
                                            case-insensitive)
    ;undead_bounty dangerous nedum       - e.g. matches "nedum vereri"

  The creature name (if given) is checked against the same undead creature
  list ;ebestiary/gameobj-data.xml uses before the script ever leaves the
  guild - if nothing undead matches, it errors out immediately instead of
  burning a Bounty Boost. A typo gets a distinct error from a real creature
  that just isn't undead (checked against every known creature name, not
  only the undead ones).

  Gives up after 25 rerolls without a match, in case of a bad creature name
  or a bounty that can't be assigned at your level.

  Whenever the script ends - success, failure, or the attempt cap - it takes
  you back to whatever room you were standing in when you started it.

  Before requesting a new bounty, it also checks whether the CURRENT one is
  already complete (`bounty` reports "You succeeded in your task and should
  report back to..."). If so, it turns that one in first - report to the
  guard, then the taskmaster, then deposits the reward at the bank - before
  going on to reroll for a new one. Handy if you run this in between hunting
  runs and don't want to babysit turn-ins yourself.

  It also checks whether your CURRENT in-progress bounty already qualifies
  (undead, and matches the creature filter if you gave one) before touching
  anything - if it does, the script leaves it alone and stops immediately
  instead of tossing out an already-good, partially-completed bounty for a
  reroll.

  If the taskmaster says there are no tasks of the requested type suitable
  for you (usually because the hunting ground has become too easy for your
  level), the script stops with a clear message instead of burning through
  the attempt cap uselessly.

  changelog:
    1.5
      - fail gracefully with a clear message when the taskmaster has no
        suitable tasks left for your level, instead of retrying uselessly
    1.4
      - check the current in-progress bounty first: if it already qualifies
        (undead + matches the filter), leave it alone instead of rerolling
        over it and losing progress
    1.3
      - turn in an already-completed bounty (guard -> taskmaster -> bank
        deposit) before requesting a new one
    1.2
      - go2 back to the room you started in when the script ends, for any
        reason (success, failure, or hitting the attempt cap)
    1.1
      - add a 25-attempt cap to the reroll loop
      - distinguish "no such creature" (typo) from "known creature, not
        undead" when validating the optional creature filter
    1.0
      - initial release

=end

module UndeadBounty
  BOUNTY_TYPES = %w[kill dangerous].freeze
  BOUNTY_MASTER_NAME = 'taskmaster'.freeze
  GUARD_NAME_PATTERN = /(?:guard|sergeant|guardsman|sentry|tavernkeeper|alchemist|purser)/i.freeze
  GUARD_NOT_READY_PATTERN = /is in no condition to talk to you/i.freeze

  # debug aid: print every bounty the script received this run, on exit.
  # on for now while we're still shaking bugs out.
  LOG_BOUNTY_HISTORY = true

  def self.bounty_history
    @bounty_history ||= []
  end

  def self.record_bounty(creature:, number:, type:, location:, verdict:)
    bounty_history << {
      creature: creature,
      number: number,
      type: type,
      location: location,
      verdict: verdict
    }
  end

  def self.print_bounty_history
    return unless LOG_BOUNTY_HISTORY
    return if bounty_history.empty?

    respond ''
    respond "UndeadBounty: bounty history for this run (#{bounty_history.length}):"
    bounty_history.each_with_index do |entry, i|
      respond format(
        '  %<n>2d. %<count>3s x %<creature>-24s [%<type>s] near %<location>-20s - %<verdict>s',
        n: i + 1,
        count: entry[:number] || '?',
        creature: entry[:creature] || 'unknown',
        type: entry[:type] || '?',
        location: entry[:location] || '?',
        verdict: entry[:verdict]
      )
    end
  end

  def self.find_npc(pattern)
    (GameObj.npcs.to_a + GameObj.room_desc.to_a).find { |n| n.status != 'dead' && n.name =~ pattern }
  end

  # tries the current room only; returns nil (does not exit) if not found,
  # so callers can move on and try another room
  def self.find_npc_in_room(pattern, retries: 6, delay: 0.25)
    retries.times do
      npc = find_npc(pattern)
      return npc if npc

      sleep delay
    end
    nil
  end

  def self.wait_for_npc(pattern, label)
    npc = find_npc_in_room(pattern, retries: 10)
    if npc.nil?
      respond "UndeadBounty: couldn't find #{label} in this room, stopping."
      exit
    end
    npc
  end

  def self.wait_for_bounty
    30.times do |i|
      task = Bounty.current
      return task if task && yield(task)

      sleep 0.25
    end
    nil
  end

  # explicitly asks the game for current bounty status instead of trusting
  # whatever XMLData.bounty_task was last left at (could be stale/unset at
  # the start of a fresh script run)
  def self.refresh_bounty_status
    fput 'bounty'
    waitrt?
    wait_for_bounty { true }
  end

  # sends a command once (no auto-resend) and watches incoming lines for up
  # to `timeout` seconds for any of the given patterns; returns the matching
  # pattern, or nil if none showed up in time
  def self.fput_and_check(command, patterns, timeout: 5)
    Script.current.clear
    fput command
    end_time = Time.now + timeout
    while Time.now < end_time
      line = get?
      if line.nil?
        sleep 0.1
      elsif (pattern = patterns.find { |p| line =~ p })
        return pattern
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # polls npc.status (mutated in place by Lich as server updates arrive)
  # until it's no longer sleeping, or timeout is hit
  def self.wait_for_npc_awake(npc, timeout: 120)
    return unless npc.status.to_s =~ /sleep/i

    respond 'UndeadBounty: guard is sleeping, waiting for them to wake up...'
    end_time = Time.now + timeout
    while Time.now < end_time && npc.status.to_s =~ /sleep/i
      sleep 1
    end
  end

  # find the town's guard room(s) generically: any room tagged 'advguard' (or
  # 'advguard2') whose nearest 'town'-tagged room matches the given town
  # name, sorted closest-to-current-position first. Some towns have more
  # than one (e.g. Wehnimer's Landing has the gate guard AND a guard inside
  # Helga's Tavern), so callers should try each in turn.
  def self.resolve_guard_rooms(town_name)
    return [] if town_name.to_s.empty?

    town_room = Room.list.find { |r| r.tags.include?('town') && r.location.to_s.include?(town_name) }
    return [] unless town_room

    candidates = Room.list.find_all do |r|
      r.tags.any? { |t| t =~ /^advguard/ } && r.find_nearest_by_tag('town') == town_room.id
    end
    return [] if candidates.empty?

    candidates.map(&:id).sort_by { |id| Room.current.path_to(id)&.length || Float::INFINITY }
  end

  def self.go_to_advguild
    Script.run('go2', 'advguild')
    waitrt?
  end

  def self.boost_bounty
    if Effects::Buffs.active?('Bounty Boost')
      respond 'UndeadBounty: Bounty Boost already active, skipping "boost bounty".'
      return
    end

    result = dothistimeout('boost bounty', 5, /You do not have any Bounty Boosts to redeem\.|You have activated a Bounty Boost\./)
    waitrt?

    if result =~ /You do not have any Bounty Boosts to redeem/
      respond "UndeadBounty: #{result}"
      exit
    end

    # the Buffs dialog data lags a bit behind the chat confirmation, so give
    # it a few seconds to catch up before treating it as a real failure
    active = false
    12.times do
      active = Effects::Buffs.active?('Bounty Boost')
      break if active

      sleep 0.5
    end

    unless active
      respond 'UndeadBounty: Bounty Boost does not appear to be active after "boost bounty", stopping.'
      exit
    end
  end

  ALREADY_ASSIGNED_PATTERN = /already been assigned a task/i.freeze
  TASKMASTER_BUSY_PATTERN = /kind of busy right now/i.freeze
  NO_SUITABLE_TASK_PATTERN = /don't seem to have any tasks that are suitable for you/i.freeze

  def self.ask_master_for_bounty(bounty_type, max_attempts: 10)
    go_to_advguild
    boost_bounty

    max_attempts.times do
      matched = fput_and_check(
        "ask #{BOUNTY_MASTER_NAME} for bounty #{bounty_type}",
        [ALREADY_ASSIGNED_PATTERN, TASKMASTER_BUSY_PATTERN, NO_SUITABLE_TASK_PATTERN]
      )
      waitrt?

      if matched == NO_SUITABLE_TASK_PATTERN
        respond "UndeadBounty: taskmaster has no #{bounty_type} tasks suitable for you here - this hunting"
        respond 'ground has probably become too easy for your level. Try another town, or a different'
        respond 'bounty type, stopping.'
        exit
      elsif matched == ALREADY_ASSIGNED_PATTERN
        respond 'UndeadBounty: taskmaster says a task is already assigned, requesting removal...'
        ask_master_for_removal
        next
      elsif matched == TASKMASTER_BUSY_PATTERN
        respond 'UndeadBounty: taskmaster is busy, retrying in 3 seconds...'
        sleep 3
        next
      end

      task = wait_for_bounty(&:assigned?)
      return task if task
    end

    respond 'UndeadBounty: never got a new assignment from the bounty master, stopping.'
    exit
  end

  def self.ask_master_for_removal
    go_to_advguild
    2.times do
      fput "ask #{BOUNTY_MASTER_NAME} for removal"
      waitrt?
    end
  end

  GUARD_TASK_COMPLETE_PATTERN = /successfully completed your task/i.freeze
  TASKMASTER_TURN_IN_PATTERN = /Good job/i.freeze

  # if a previous run already finished killing/gathering/etc. and just never
  # got turned in, `bounty` reports "You succeeded in your task and should
  # report back to..." (Bounty.current.type == :guard). Handle that before
  # starting a new reroll loop: report to the guard, then the taskmaster,
  # then bank the reward.
  def self.turn_in_bounty(task)
    respond 'UndeadBounty: found a completed bounty waiting to be turned in, handling that first...'

    npc = locate_guard(task.town)
    confirmed = false
    5.times do
      wait_for_npc_awake(npc)
      matched = fput_and_check("ask #{npc.noun} about bounty", [GUARD_TASK_COMPLETE_PATTERN, GUARD_NOT_READY_PATTERN])
      waitrt?

      if matched == GUARD_NOT_READY_PATTERN
        respond "UndeadBounty: #{npc.noun} isn't able to talk right now, waiting and retrying..."
        sleep 5
        next
      end

      confirmed = !matched.nil?
      break
    end

    unless confirmed
      respond "UndeadBounty: #{npc.noun} didn't confirm the completed task, stopping so you can check manually."
      exit
    end

    go_to_advguild
    confirmed = false
    5.times do
      matched = fput_and_check("ask #{BOUNTY_MASTER_NAME} about bounty", [TASKMASTER_TURN_IN_PATTERN, TASKMASTER_BUSY_PATTERN])
      waitrt?

      if matched == TASKMASTER_BUSY_PATTERN
        respond 'UndeadBounty: taskmaster is busy, retrying in 3 seconds...'
        sleep 3
        next
      end

      confirmed = !matched.nil?
      break
    end

    unless confirmed
      respond 'UndeadBounty: taskmaster never confirmed the turn-in, stopping so you can check manually.'
      exit
    end

    respond 'UndeadBounty: bounty turned in, depositing at the bank...'
    Script.run('go2', 'bank')
    waitrt?
    fput 'deposit all'
    waitrt?
  end

  # checks status ONCE before touching anything, and handles whatever's
  # already there:
  #   - a completed task waiting on a guard report -> turn it in
  #   - an in-progress task that already qualifies (undead + matches the
  #     filter) -> leave it alone, don't burn it on a reroll
  #   - anything else (:none, or an in-progress task that doesn't qualify)
  #     -> nothing to do here, let the normal reroll loop handle it
  # returns :already_matching if the in-progress bounty already qualifies,
  # so run() knows to stop instead of starting the reroll loop.
  def self.handle_existing_bounty(creature_filter)
    task = refresh_bounty_status
    return unless task

    if task.type == :guard
      turn_in_bounty(task)
      return
    end

    return unless task.creature

    creature = task.creature
    return unless creature_undead?(creature) && creature_matches_filter?(creature, creature_filter)

    record_bounty(creature: creature, number: task.number, type: task.type, location: task.location,
                  verdict: 'UNDEAD - already in progress, left as-is')
    respond "UndeadBounty: current bounty is already #{task.number || '?'} x #{creature} in/near " \
            "#{task.location} - UNDEAD#{" and matches '#{creature_filter}'" if creature_filter}, leaving it alone."
    :already_matching
  end

  # remembers which guard room actually had the guard, per town, so later
  # loop iterations don't have to re-check every candidate room every time
  def self.guard_room_cache
    @guard_room_cache ||= {}
  end

  # travels to the given town's guard room(s) (using the cache, then the
  # generic tag-based search, same as before) and returns the guard npc.
  # exits if no guard can be found anywhere.
  def self.locate_guard(town)
    npc = nil
    found_room = nil
    cached_room = guard_room_cache[town]

    if cached_room
      Script.run('go2', cached_room.to_s)
      waitrt?
      npc = find_npc_in_room(GUARD_NAME_PATTERN)
      found_room = cached_room if npc
    end

    unless npc
      room_ids = resolve_guard_rooms(town)
      if room_ids.empty?
        respond "UndeadBounty: couldn't find a guard room for '#{town}', falling back to the nearest advguard tag."
        room_ids = ['advguard']
      end

      room_ids.each do |room_id|
        next if room_id == cached_room # already tried above

        Script.run('go2', room_id.to_s)
        waitrt?
        npc = find_npc_in_room(GUARD_NAME_PATTERN)
        if npc
          found_room = room_id
          break
        end

        respond "UndeadBounty: no guard found at room #{room_id}, trying the next location..."
      end
    end

    unless npc
      respond "UndeadBounty: couldn't find the town guard at any known location for '#{town}', stopping."
      exit
    end

    # only cache real room ids, not the 'advguard' tag fallback
    guard_room_cache[town] = found_room if found_room.is_a?(Integer)
    npc
  end

  def self.ask_guard_for_bounty(assignment)
    npc = locate_guard(assignment.town)

    task = nil
    10.times do
      wait_for_npc_awake(npc)

      matched = fput_and_check("ask #{npc.noun} for bounty", [GUARD_NOT_READY_PATTERN])
      waitrt?

      if matched
        respond "UndeadBounty: #{npc.noun} isn't able to talk right now, waiting and retrying..."
        sleep 5
        next
      end

      task = wait_for_bounty(&:ready?)
      break if task
    end

    unless task
      respond "UndeadBounty: guard never gave a creature assignment, stopping."
      exit
    end
    task
  end

  # same technique ;ebestiary uses: build a throwaway GameObj and read its
  # type string out of data/gameobj-data.xml
  def self.creature_undead?(creature_name)
    return false if creature_name.to_s.empty?

    noun = creature_name.split(' ').last
    fakeobj = GameObj.new(666_999, noun, creature_name)
    fakeobj.type.to_s.split(',').include?('undead')
  end

  # each type's 'name' pattern is one big regex: ^(?:adjective prefixes)?(name1|name2|...)$
  # pull the literal creature names out of the middle group so we can search
  # them, rather than matching against it directly.
  def self.creature_names_from_pattern(pattern)
    source = pattern&.source.to_s
    return [] if source.empty?

    boundary = source.index(')?(')
    body = boundary ? source[(boundary + 3)..-1] : source
    body = body.sub(/\)\$\z/, '')

    body.split('|').map { |n| n.delete('\\') }.reject(&:empty?)
  end

  def self.known_undead_creatures
    return @known_undead_creatures if defined?(@known_undead_creatures)

    GameObj.load_data if GameObj.type_data.nil? || GameObj.type_data.empty?
    @known_undead_creatures = creature_names_from_pattern(GameObj.type_data.dig('undead', :name))
  end

  # same idea as known_undead_creatures, but across every type (armor, food,
  # every creature type, etc.) - used only to tell a plain typo apart from a
  # real, known creature that just isn't undead.
  def self.known_creatures
    return @known_creatures if defined?(@known_creatures)

    GameObj.load_data if GameObj.type_data.nil? || GameObj.type_data.empty?
    names = []
    GameObj.type_data.each_value { |fields| names.concat(creature_names_from_pattern(fields[:name])) }
    @known_creatures = names.uniq
  end

  # partial, case-insensitive search against the known undead creature list
  def self.find_undead_matches(query)
    return [] if query.to_s.strip.empty?

    q = query.strip.downcase
    known_undead_creatures.select { |name| name.downcase.include?(q) }
  end

  # same, but against every creature name gameobj-data.xml knows about,
  # regardless of type - for typo detection
  def self.find_creature_matches(query)
    return [] if query.to_s.strip.empty?

    q = query.strip.downcase
    known_creatures.select { |name| name.downcase.include?(q) }
  end

  def self.creature_matches_filter?(creature_name, filter)
    return true if filter.to_s.strip.empty?

    creature_name.to_s.downcase.include?(filter.strip.downcase)
  end

  # max rerolls before giving up - guards against an endless loop if the
  # creature name has a typo, or it's a bounty that just can't be assigned
  # (e.g. wrong level range)
  MAX_ATTEMPTS = 25

  def self.run(bounty_type, creature_filter = nil)
    # module-level state survives across separate script runs in the same
    # Lich session, so reset it at the start of each run
    @bounty_history = []
    start_room = Room.current.id

    return if handle_existing_bounty(creature_filter) == :already_matching

    attempt = 0

    loop do
      attempt += 1
      if attempt > MAX_ATTEMPTS
        respond "UndeadBounty: hit the #{MAX_ATTEMPTS}-attempt limit without landing on a match, stopping."
        respond 'UndeadBounty: double check the creature name/spelling, or this bounty may not be assignable at your level.'
        return
      end

      assignment = ask_master_for_bounty(bounty_type)
      task = ask_guard_for_bounty(assignment)

      unless task.creature
        record_bounty(creature: nil, number: task.number, type: task.type, location: task.location,
                       verdict: "couldn't parse creature from guard response")
        respond "UndeadBounty: could not determine the bounty creature from the guard's response, stopping."
        return
      end

      creature = task.creature
      respond "UndeadBounty: assigned #{task.number || '?'} x #{creature} in/near #{task.location}."

      if creature_undead?(creature) && creature_matches_filter?(creature, creature_filter)
        record_bounty(creature: creature, number: task.number, type: task.type, location: task.location,
                       verdict: 'UNDEAD - stopped here')
        respond "UndeadBounty: #{creature} is UNDEAD#{" and matches '#{creature_filter}'" if creature_filter} - stopping, take it from here."
        return
      end

      verdict = creature_undead?(creature) ? "undead, but doesn't match filter '#{creature_filter}' - rerolled" : 'not undead - rerolled'
      record_bounty(creature: creature, number: task.number, type: task.type, location: task.location, verdict: verdict)

      respond "UndeadBounty: #{creature} doesn't qualify, requesting removal and rerolling..."
      ask_master_for_removal
    end
  ensure
    print_bounty_history
    return_to_room(start_room)
  end

  def self.return_to_room(room_id)
    return if room_id.nil? || Room.current.id == room_id

    respond "UndeadBounty: heading back to your starting room (#{room_id})..."
    Script.run('go2', room_id.to_s)
    waitrt?
  end

  def self.show_help
    respond 'usage:'
    respond "    ;#{Script.current.name} help                  - show this help"
    respond "    ;#{Script.current.name} kill                  - loop requesting \"kill\" (culling) bounties,"
    respond '                                            stop on any undead assignment'
    respond "    ;#{Script.current.name} dangerous             - same, but for \"dangerous\" bounties"
    respond "    ;#{Script.current.name} kill tomb wight       - stop only when the assigned creature is"
    respond '                                            undead AND matches "tomb wight" (partial,'
    respond '                                            case-insensitive)'
    respond "    ;#{Script.current.name} dangerous nedum       - e.g. matches \"nedum vereri\""
    respond ''
    respond 'The optional creature name is checked against the known undead creature list'
    respond 'before the script ever leaves the guild - if nothing undead matches, it errors'
    respond 'out immediately instead of burning a Bounty Boost.'
    respond ''
    respond 'Boosts a bounty (if needed), asks the taskmaster for a new task, follows the'
    respond "assignment to the assigned town's guard to learn the creature, and stops as"
    respond 'soon as that creature is undead. Otherwise it requests removal and rerolls.'
    respond ''
    respond "Gives up after #{UndeadBounty::MAX_ATTEMPTS} rerolls without a match, in case the"
    respond 'creature/spelling is wrong or the bounty just cannot be assigned at your level.'
    respond ''
    respond 'When the script ends for any reason, it takes you back to the room you started in.'
    respond ''
    respond 'If your current bounty is already complete when the script starts, it turns that'
    respond 'one in first (guard, then taskmaster, then a bank deposit) before requesting a new one.'
    respond ''
    respond "If your current bounty is still in progress but already qualifies, it's left alone"
    respond "instead of being tossed out for a reroll - progress on it isn't lost."
    respond ''
    respond 'If the taskmaster has no suitable tasks left for your level (hunting ground got too'
    respond 'easy), the script stops with a clear message instead of retrying uselessly.'
  end
end

## script.vars[0] is the entire raw argument string; the individual
## space-split tokens start at script.vars[1].
if script.vars[1].to_s.strip =~ /^help$/i
  UndeadBounty.show_help
  exit
end

bounty_type = (script.vars[1] || 'kill').downcase
unless UndeadBounty::BOUNTY_TYPES.include?(bounty_type)
  respond "UndeadBounty: unknown bounty type '#{bounty_type}', expected one of: #{UndeadBounty::BOUNTY_TYPES.join(', ')}"
  exit
end

creature_filter = script.vars[2..-1].to_a.join(' ').strip
creature_filter = nil if creature_filter.empty?

if creature_filter
  matches = UndeadBounty.find_undead_matches(creature_filter)
  if matches.empty?
    # same technique ;ebestiary uses to look creatures up: if it doesn't
    # match ANY known creature name, it's probably a typo; if it matches
    # something that just isn't undead, say so instead
    any_matches = UndeadBounty.find_creature_matches(creature_filter)
    if any_matches.empty?
      respond "UndeadBounty: '#{creature_filter}' doesn't match any known creature name - check your spelling, stopping."
    else
      respond "UndeadBounty: '#{creature_filter}' matches #{any_matches.first(5).join(', ')}#{' and others' if any_matches.length > 5}, but none of them are undead, stopping."
    end
    exit
  end
  respond "UndeadBounty: targeting creatures matching '#{creature_filter}' (e.g. #{matches.first(5).join(', ')})#{' and others' if matches.length > 5}"
end

UndeadBounty.run(bounty_type, creature_filter)
